{
  "requirement": "`Sources::parse` reads a single file snapshot. `OutlineContent` retains its\nidentity and parsed contents under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.provider_results.parse_snapshot`.\n\nInspect the selectable items in a visible project file. Pass a returned selector\nand the same `path` to `read_source` to read that item. Follow [`next`](#continuing-a-paged-result) to see more items.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `outline_source.behavior`. I read the `dispatch` implementation for `outline_source` and `Sources::parse` only to see how the outline output is built. I derived the expected behavior from the requirement text.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **Outline a visible project file as the selectable items of one parsed snapshot.**\n   - `fixed_tools_deliver_domain_contents_with_mandatory_snapshot_dependencies` outlines a plain-text file and a Rust file. For the Rust file it asserts the exact item list, covering the root, `fn[chosen]`, `impl[Thing]` and nested `impl[Thing]/fn[nested]`.\n   - `source_outlines_present_every_top_level_and_nested_item` asserts the full ordered list of top-level and nested items.\n   - `source_tools_forward_arguments_and_present_discovery_and_read_results` also asserts a nested `fn[chosen]` entry.\n\n2. **\"Visible\" files only.**\n   - `outline_tool_rejects_excluded_and_unreadable_sources_without_domain_reads` outlines an allowed file successfully.\n   - The same test asserts errors for a `.gitignore`d file, `../` traversal, `.git/config`, a missing file, a non-string path, and an unparseable `invalid.rs`.\n   - It also asserts that no domain reads happen.\n\n3. **`OutlineContent` retains identity and parsed contents (`parse_snapshot`).**\n   - The outline response carries the file identity: tests assert `page[\"path\"] == \"code.rs\"`, or the `resource` value in the `SourceTools` variant.\n   - `source_outlines_present_every_top_level_and_nested_item` checks `parsed.resource == id` and `parsed.source.text() == text`.\n   - Item lists come from the parsed outline of the same file, as the nested and plain-line assertions show.\n   - `cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input` also exercises the retained snapshot, since it outlines a parsed source and then reads items from it.\n\n4. **A returned selector and the same `path` passed to `read_source` read that item.**\n   - `outline_continuations_keep_the_file_and_complete_ordered_items` takes the last selector from the paged outline of a 400-item file. It reads it with the same `path` and asserts the returned node and text. It also asserts that the domain read of `code.rs#fn[item_399]` happened.\n   - `source_tools_forward_arguments_and_present_discovery_and_read_results` reads the root and `fn[chosen]` using outline-style addresses.\n\n5. **Follow `next` to see more items.**\n   - `outline_continuations_keep_the_file_and_complete_ordered_items` follows `next` through more than one page.\n   - It asserts that each page is non-empty, that `path` is stable across pages, and that the concatenated items equal the complete ordered expected list.\n   - `source_discovery_and_its_pages_do_not_register_reads` also follows outline pages and checks that they add no dependencies.\n\n**Gaps considered**\n\n- There is no test that edits a file between two reads of the same parse. The text says `Sources::parse` reads a single snapshot. `Sources::parse` does one `read` and builds the `OutlineContent` from that text, so the single-snapshot behavior is exercised through every outline test. I did not count this as a missing scenario.\n- Dependency behavior and `read_source` fingerprints belong to other requirements and are not assessed here.\n\nAll tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. I found no unaddressed obligation, wrong expectation, or weak assertion.",
